   177	It broke back a yard, and the wheels dropped into the gap, and climbed again. It would be so the whole way, he saw: the ice would give under that weight plate by plate until the wheels found something thick enough to bear them. The question was only which way the thick lay. He had the read under his chest and his forearms now, where it gave him the same pace and a half that it gave his boots, and he crawled backward before the mules' knees, feeling.
   178	
   179	"Left," he said. "Seln. A yard left. It's rotten on your right as far as I can reach."
   180	
   181	Seln brought their heads round, and the rope swung with them. In the cart's bed a lashing had parted; a barrel came loose from the dozen, and rolled down the slant of the boards, and went over the tailboard into the black water without any sound that he could hear above the mules.
   182	
   183	"Stand!"
   184	
   185	The boy's voice went up the bank, and the drum stopped. The cart hung on its wheels, half out. Cael crawled, and felt, and found the place where his weight began to go away evenly on both sides again.
   186	
   187	"Here. It's sound from here. Bring them to me."
   188	
   189	"Walk!"
   190	
   191	He could see almost nothing by then. The snow a foot from his face was a white blur; he could not have counted his own fingers. He had never held the read for half so long, or leaned on it so hard, and it had taken his near sight from him piece by piece as the price. He went on backward on his elbows with his eyes shut, since they were of no use open, and called the ground to a man whom he could hear and could not see. *Left. Straight. Stand. Walk.* The boy sent each word up the bank, and up there a girl with one arm walked a mule round a drum and stopped her dead at every *stand*.
   192	
   193	He was told afterward how it had gone in the shed. Lira could not see the river from the horse-walk, or so much as the door. She had gone round that ring backward, with the mule's nose against her chest and her right hand shut on the cheek-strap, hearing two words shouted over and over by a boy whose voice was breaking; and each time that the second came she had set her heels and stopped a beast three times her own weight with a word. Her left arm had lain in its sling throughout and held nothing. The ferryman had turned on his keg to watch her do it.
   194	
   195	Then there was a boom under him, like a great door shutting, and the whole sheet rang.
   196	
   197	"She's up," said Seln. "All four wheels. They are on the green."
   198	
   199	Cael let the read go.
   200	
   201	He lay on his face on two hands of blue ice and breathed. His left leg was wet to the knee, where a plate had let him through as he went down, and the cold of it had begun to bite; and his head felt like a room from which the lamp has been carried out. He heard the cart mules blowing. He heard a ferryman's rope go slack and fall across eighty yards of snow.
   202	
   203	It was Brom who got him onto his feet, by the collar, with the one arm.
   204	
   205	"Can you see me?"
   206	
   207	"I can see that you're large."
   208	
   209	"That'll do till supper."
   210	
   211	They carried the carter up to the lower inn on a hurdle. He was alive, and swearing in a whisper at his own mules, and the keeper had him by the hearth in a blanket before anybody had asked whose he was. Nobody did ask. He was a man off the ice; in that country it was all the name that anyone required of him.
   212	
   213	The ferryman had come down off his keg at last. He stood at the shed's door and looked at his long line, lying wet and black across the river, and at the brown mule, who was eating straw.
   214	
   215	"That's my rope," he said.
   216	
   217	"It's what fetched him out," said Lira.
   218	
   219	"It was dry. It'll want two nights by a fire now, and turning, and coiling again as I had it."
   220	
   221	"We'll turn it," said Brom.
   222	
   223	"You will." He considered the mule. "She's wound a cart out of my river."
   224	
   225	"She has."
   226	
   227	"On my drum. In my shed." His eye had gone to a reed hurdle with a hole in it the size of a fist, and stayed there for a good while. He named no figure. He went back to his keg, and took up a block, and began to work tallow into it with his thumb.
   228	
   229	"He will," said Brom, very low.
   230	
   231	---
   232	
   233	Pike had no slip for it, and he said so that night under his cloth.
   234	
   235	"Nobody posted it. There's no hirer, and no price, and I've nothing to write a line on." He scratched his stump. Then he turned his shoulder to the room regardless, and gave it out twice, in the voice that he kept for the plank: a cart through the ice at the bend; a man and two beasts and a load brought off, less one barrel of fish; nobody lost. "That's said. It's not entered. I'd have you know the difference."
   236	
   237	Cael wrote it in the store behind the chimney, with a blanket round him and his left foot in a bucket of warm water. He could see the page again, though the lines of it still wandered.
   238	
   239	*Hundred and sixty-first day. A cart through the river at the bend, eighty yards out. The carter, his two mules and the cart brought off on the ferryman's line, wound in on his drum by Oryn's mule, with Lira leading her. Brom fetched the man out lying down, left-handed. Seln had the beasts.*
   240	
   241	*The read: the ground, on ice, from the shelf to the hole and back to the green. In earnest. All my near sight, and the last of it done on my face.*
   242	
   243	*Deployment since the Line: twenty-nine.*
   244	
   245	Lira had brought him the bowl herself, and she stood in the doorway while he wrote, turning her right hand over to look at the palm, which had a cheek-strap's print across it.
   246	
   247	"I never saw any of it," she said. "I heard a boy shout two words forty times, and I walked a mule in a ring. That's my whole account of the thing."
   248	
   249	"You stopped her dead, every time. He'd have had those wheels in the hole again if you'd been a stride late."
   250	
   251	"She's a mule. You tell her, and she does it." Lira shut the hand. "It's a good deal more than I could say of some people on a count."
   252	
   253	He turned to the other leaf, and filled a line that he had ruled twenty-eight days ago.
   254	
   255	*Iron, from Brom: the read. The mass of a thing and which way it goes. The ground or the air, never both. A pace and a half and not a hand more, so that to know a place I have to go and stand on it. Sound ice takes a weight evenly all round; rotten ice takes it on one side. Frozen ground rings. Leaned on for long, it bills the eyes: an hour after a calf, and more tonight.*
   256	
   257	*On the ice the other nine were no use. Anchor would have held the lip and cost me the read. Wind would have saved me and nobody else. Compression would have put the load into the thing we were standing on.*
   258	
   259	*Two still to try: Storm and Anchor. Tide waits on somebody's yes.*
   260	
   261	On the far side of the lamp Karis had moved her three heaps to the table's end. She had the kit's case open before her, with the cord out of it, and beside the cord a peg of peeled hazel, cut to the length of the iron stake and pointed with a knife. Under her two hands, open for the first time since Seln had put it into them on a road, was the dead surveyor's book.
   262	
   263	"I have left this for thirty-four days," she said. "I did not wish to read another person's findings until I had finished entering my own. I have finished."
   264	
   265	She drew the cord toward her as she spoke. It was a plain thing, of tarred hemp no thicker than a pencil, with a knot tied in it at every fifth stride and a brass eye whipped into one end, where a stake would go through to hold it to the ground. A man had measured a circle and the floor inside it with nothing more than that, and a mallet, and his own two feet.
   266	
   267	She did not read Cael the distances. She laid one finger at the head of the book's first page, above everything else that was written there.
   268	
   269	"He begins with this. Four signs in a row, and after them the number, thirty-two. It is how he names the place that he is standing in." Her finger did not move. "They are a clerk's signs. They are not a carver's; I have forty-one kinds of mark from three floors, and these are not cut like any of them. Seln read registry paper for fifteen years, and he does not know the index that they are taken from."
   270	
   271	"What do they say?"
   272	
   273	"I cannot read them. I can tell you only that somebody could. A person sat at a desk, and gave that hole in the ground four signs, before this man ever carried a mallet down into it." She turned the leaf. There was one line of pencil on the second page, and a stroke going off toward the edge. "And here is where he stopped."
   274	
   275	*third site confirmed on bearing*
   276	
   277	Cael bent to it. The words went in and out of each other like fish in a pool, and he sat back.
   278	
   279	"I can't make out a letter of it tonight."
   280	
   281	"Then it will wait for your eyes." She shut the book, and ran the cord through her fingers to the first knot. "On the hundred and sixty-eighth I shall want all five of you in the inn yard at noon, with dry hands. I have had this cord for forty-six days and I have not yet seen anybody measure with it. It is time that we learned how he did."

