   277	He had known for a long while that the sentence would end so. It did not help him at all to have been right.
   278	
   279	"And somebody would have to walk in."
   280	
   281	"Somebody would. I am asking nothing of you tonight. I am telling you what I shall be arguing, and from what, so that you need not hear it for the first time in front of the others."
   282	
   283	"There's one who hasn't seen the map," he said. "It was hers to see first; she wrote so. Say it when she's at the table."
   284	
   285	"After Thornwater, then," said Karis. "To six."
   286	
   287	The carter came over the saddle the next morning with his breeches wet to the thigh. The road was open, he said, if a person did not mind the ford.
   288	
   289	Lira had been ready since before it was light. The bow was on her back, with its one bolt, and the sling was knotted high at her collar-bone as a healer had left it at a fork. She had not spoken of that arm since a strip of bark was read out at a hearth, and she did not speak of it now. She went up the saddle road first, on the whole of her foot, and the rest of them followed her.

