     3	They came down to Thornwater's ford in the forenoon of the two hundred and sixty-fifth day, and Cael did not at first know the place.
     4	
     5	He had crossed it a good many times. There had been a ledge of grey rock from bank to bank, ten feet broad, with the river sliding over it no deeper than a shin, and a row of white stones to show a wheel where the rock ended. There had been a bar of gravel in the middle that a person could stand on; he had fought on that bar, and had seen a hound drowned beside it.
     6	
     7	There was a brown river now, fifty yards wide and going like a mill-race, with the white stones under it and the bar under it as well. Nothing showed of thirty yards of gravel but a long seam in the current, where the water humped and ran on. It had brought down a whole cold term of snow from the hills, and it made a noise that he could feel in the shingle through his boots.
     8	
     9	"He said that he didn't mind it," said Brom. "The carter. I'd like to know what he does mind."
    10	
    11	Karis had the hide out of her coat already. She wrapped it in her spare shirt, and then in Seln's oilcloth, and lashed the roll on top of the mule's pack with her own hands, as high as it would go.
    12	
    13	"If she goes down, somebody is to save that before he saves me," she said. "I am not making a joke."
    14	
    15	Cael looked at the water, and the read asked what it always asked.
    16	
    17	It could have found him that ledge. It would have come back dull and late through a yard of brown river, as it had through the water of a drowned hall, but it would have told him where the rock was and where it stopped; and he had it half open before he had weighed the thing. This was a road. He had a rule for roads, and he had broken it once on a hillside, after a trial, for a calf. Two hounds had gone down this same water a long while ago and had never been accounted for.
    18	
    19	And there was a person standing on the shingle beside him, a Wind of the first rank in Iron, who had been finding floors with her feet since before he had anything in his own.
    20	
    21	"Lira," he said. "Where's the rock?"
    22	
    23	She had been looking at the river ever since they came out of the scrub, and she took her time over the answer.
    24	
    25	"There." She pointed with her chin, upstream of the seam. "See where it's smooth? It's quick, and it's got a skin on it, like oil. That's water going over something level. Below that it breaks up; there's a line of little standing waves, all the way across. That's where it falls off the edge." She squinted. "So the ledge is the smooth, and it's as wide as the smooth is, and the waves are where you die."
    26	
    27	"And the grooves?"
    28	
    29	"I'll find those with my boot. They're a hand deep. Carts have been cutting them since before anybody's grandmother." She sat down on a stone and pulled the laces of the great boot as tight as they would draw. "I go first. You put your feet where mine were. Nobody walks on the downstream side of the mule."
    30	
    31	Brom had the box-rope uncoiled. He did not ask her leave; he passed it round her waist, and knotted it, and took three turns of the other end about his own left forearm.
    32	
    33	"That's for me," he said. "I'll walk easier."
    34	
    35	She let him. She went into the river on the whole of her foot, as she had gone everywhere for the best part of two hundred days, and at the third stride it had her to the knee, and at the sixth to the thigh. Cael saw her stop, and feel about with the big boot, and shift a little upstream.
    36	
    37	"Got it," she called. "It's slimed. Walk flat."
    38	
    39	They went over in her line: Brom at the rope's length, with the mule's cheek-strap in the same fist, and Karis on the upstream side with both hands shut on the pack-girth, and Cael, and Seln last. The river leaned on them the whole way, and it was so cold that it had no feeling in it after the first breath. He kept his left boot in a groove that he could not see, and his eyes on the back of Lira's neck, and the read shut.
    40	
    41	Half way over the first channel the big boot turned under her.
    42	
    43	He saw it go: the right ankle, the one that nobody had ever mended, rolling outward on the green. Her right arm flew wide. Under the grey wool her left started to come, and stopped at a knot, and the rope came taut in Brom's fist.
    44	
    45	She did not go down. She stood on that foot, with her teeth showing and the brown water piling against her thigh, until it had agreed to be a foot again; and then she set it flat, and went on.
    46	
    47	They came up onto the far shingle streaming, a little before noon. She sat down where the stones were dry and reached for the ankle with both hands; she had only the one to hold it with, and he saw her remember that.
    48	
    49	"It held nothing," said Brom.
    50	
    51	"It held nothing," said Lira. "I'm getting tired of saying it."
    52	
    53	---
    54	
    55	The bar of Thornwater's eastern gate was drawn for them before they had called, and on the platform beside it a lean man lifted two fingers from the stave of a bow.
    56	
    57	Lira put up her chin to him. It was not much, and from her it was a salute.
    58	
    59	The steading's woman had the bar in her two hands. She counted them in through her gate with her lips, as she had on the first night that they ever stood in it; and Cael saw her get to five, and go on looking down the road for a sixth, before she remembered who was inside her fence already.
    60	
    61	It was the mule who found the healer. The brown beast had lifted her head as the bar dropped behind them. She drew her cheek-strap out of Brom's fist without any hurry, and walked away across the flagstones with her pack on, between the geese, to the bench under the back room's window; and there she put her nose down against a broad square back in an old coat.
    62	
    63	Oryn was on her heels at the bench's end, with an old man's hand between her own two. She did not turn round. Her right hand left its work, and went out behind her, and found a small brown cheek where it had always been.
    64	
    65	She had not asked after that animal in a hundred and forty-three days, by letter or by carter, and she did not ask now. She was a Tide, of the fourth rank in Iron, and nobody at that gate had ever asked her for the figure either.
    66	
    67	"You came by the ford," she said, to the hand that she was binding.
    68	
    69	"It's to the thigh," said Brom.
    70	
    71	"It'll be to the knee in a week. You couldn't wait a week."
    72	
    73	"No," said Lira.
    74	
    75	"No. I'd not have thought it."
    76	
    77	She tied off the linen, and sent the old man away with a word about keeping it dry, and only then got up and turned to them. Her eyes passed over Cael as they would have passed over the gatepost. They went to four yards of webbing, and to a knot upon it that she had not tied; they went down to a boot with river water running out of its lacings; and they stopped at a fold of grey wool, and stayed there.
    78	
    79	"There's a loft, and there's a pot," said Oryn. "I eat now, and I'm on my back two hours, and I'll look at nobody tonight who isn't bleeding. In the morning, before that bench fills."
    80	
    81	She had Lira on the bench on the two hundred and sixty-sixth morning, as soon as there was light to see by, with the frost still white on the flags.
    82	
    83	The other four stood by the well, since nobody had told them to stay and nobody had told them to go. Oryn came out of the back room with her sleeves pushed up. She stopped a stride and a half short of the bench, and put her hands on her hips.
    84	
    85	"I said first snow, near enough. Then I said that I'd say. Then I went north, and it snowed on the pair of us." She nodded at the wool. "How long has that been up?"
    86	
    87	Lira had the figure ready. Cael understood, hearing it, that she had had it ready every morning.
    88	
    89	"Five months and twenty days."
    90	
    91	"That's a season. Take it off."
    92	
    93	Lira's right hand went up to her collar-bone, where the knot was, and stopped there.
    94	
    95	"It's yours," she said. "You tied it. You've tied it every time."
    96	
    97	"I have. I've had my hands round that arm oftener than round most, and each time I've put it back in the wool myself, because you'd have had it out in a day if I'd left it to you." Oryn did not stir from where she stood. "I'm not putting it back. I'm not taking it out, either. It's been mine for a season. It stops being mine when you take it."
    98	
    99	Nobody in that yard moved, and on the platform the lean man had turned his back on the valley to watch.
   100	
   101	Lira looked at the healer for the length of a breath. Then she bent her head, and got the knot between her teeth, and worked at it with her right hand until it gave. The grey wool slid down. She drew it off over her head; and the arm that had lain against her breast since a white floor on the Fallow, through a stair and a hall and the whole of a cold term, came down by its own weight and hung at her side.
   102	
   103	It was as thin as a rail. It was white, and there were eight small marks along it in a row, as pale now as the skin about them. She lifted it, and it rose slowly, and shook, and the fingers opened and shut.
   104	
   105	Oryn came the stride and a half. Her thick fingers went round the forearm, overlapping, and her lips moved to ten.
   106	
   107	"All eight," she said. "They've forgotten that they were ever anything else."
   108	
   109	She let go. Lira stood with the sling in her right hand and her left hanging, and looked down at the great boot.
   110	
   111	---
   112	
   113	She had asked for that ankle once, from a mule's back, on a mountain road, and had been told that it was a tape and a season. In all the roads since then Cael had not heard her ask a living person for anything: not for a hand over a ledge, or a turn on a mule's back, or a bow spanned, though she had taken each of those when it was held out to her.
   114	
   115	"Will you mend it."
   116	
   117	It was not said as a question. She had got it out as she got out a count, level, with nothing leaning on it, and her eyes on the healer's face.
   118	
   119	Oryn regarded her. Something went over that square brown countenance which Cael had seen there only once, on the edge of a marsh, and it was gone before he could have put a name to it.
   120	
   121	"Lie still," Oryn said.
   122	
   123	She took her in to the plank. The door of the back room stood open, since that healer never shut a door on anything but a long reading, and from the well they could see a broad back, and a pair of elbows, and at the table's end a bare foot. They heard what she said first, as she said it to every person who lay down there: what she would do, and why, and what it would be like. It had knit as it lay in a river, crooked. She would have to open the way and hold it open, for the better part of an hour, and the girl would feel it going right, which was worse to feel than a thing going wrong.
   124	
   125	"I've felt it going wrong for two hundred days," said Lira's voice. "Get on."
   126	
   127	It took the hour, which Brom counted by the shadow of the well's windlass on the flags. Karis sat on the coping with the roll of hide across her knees and did not once open her notebook, and Seln stood where he could see both gates. The lean man had come down off his platform, which Cael had never known him do, and waited a little way from the door with his stave grounded, looking at nothing. Cael watched the back of a healer who was spending, on a foot, what a child at some northern gate might want of her in a week; and he knew that she had weighed that before she pushed up her sleeves.
   128	
   129	When Lira came out, she came barefoot on the right side, over frosted stone.
   130	
   131	She walked to the well, and it was not how she had walked since the fifty-third day. She went up on the ball of that foot and came down, like a girl at a rail trying a floor, and did it again. Then she stood on the right leg alone, with her two arms out, the thin one and the other; and all four of them saw her face, which she had forgotten to keep.
   132	
   133	The healer had followed her as far as the sill. She was grey about the mouth, and she had one hand on the door-post.
   134	
   135	"Thank you."
   136	
   137	Lira said it to Oryn.
   138	
   139	"Route's whole," Oryn said. "The knee held. Nobody died of three weeks."
   140	
   141	She sat down on her keg after that, with her head lowered, for a count of fifty. When she raised it she looked at the arm.
   142	
   143	"It's an arm. Use it. It'll be slow for a month and it'll ache in the wet for a year and then it won't. Don't fall on it."
   144	
   145	"And the shoulder?" said Brom. He had not meant to ask, and it came out of him sideways.
   146	
   147	She got up, and walked round him at her stride and a half, and studied four yards of webbing from the top turn to the bottom.
   148	
   149	"That's not my knot. A woman tied that who's kept shoulders shut for forty cold terms, and I can't read through her web without undoing it." She put two fingers under the lowest turn, and took them away. "She says when. I'll look at it beside her, when she's by, and you'll have what the pair of us make of it."
   150	
   151	Lira had gone back to the bench, and she was holding the great boot. It took Cael a breath to see which hand she had it in. It was the left; the arm shook with the weight of a carter's boot, and held it.
   152	
   153	"I came off a mountain in this," she said. "I went round three of those places in it, and up a wall, and over that river yesterday. There's a whole shirt of mine in the toe. I don't know what a person does with it now."
   154	
   155	"It's a boot," said Brom.
   156	
   157	"It's a trough with laces."
   158	
   159	"It's the only right one that you own. Put it on."
   160	
   161	She took the shirt out of the toe, a strip at a time. Then she put her mended foot into a boot that was two fingers too long for it, and stamped, and went across the yard to hear what a lean man on a platform had to say about a bow.
   162	
   163	---
   164	
   165	Karis unrolled the hide that evening on the plank in the back room, when the bench was empty and the healer had slept.
   166	
   167	It was dry. She set a pebble on each corner, and laid the older strip of brass along the pencil line; and Cael laid Hesk's beyond it, so that the two made one bright edge from the ring by the Fallow to the bare skin past the fourth.
   168	
   169	Oryn stood at the table's end with her sleeves down. She had ordered them, by letter, to bring that thing, and she looked at it for a good while before she spoke.
   170	
   171	"That's a line."
   172	
   173	"It is," said Karis. "You have seen three of those rings. You walked off the road at a fork before there was a fourth."
   174	
   175	She told it as she would have entered it. The fourth lay two days beyond the fork, under the end of a ridge, and it was twice the size of the others, by the bend of its edge. There was a fence of iron round it, of the registry's pattern, with a bar to every pace and one number on every bar. Something stood in the middle that five people had looked at in the dusk and could not make out.
   176	
   177	"Four hundred and forty yards, and two strides, from the iron to its middle," said Karis. "I had that by the bend, from outside. I have not one sheet of the place."
   178	
   179	"And who went in?"
   180	
   181	"Nobody," said Cael.
   182	
   183	The healer's eyes came up from the hide.
   184	
   185	"I said *not without her*. I said it at the fence, with the four of them wanting four different things. There was nobody there to stand at the edge with her sleeves up, and nobody knew what a circle of that size would do to a Path. So we counted the bars, and came home."
   186	
   187	"You kept five people on the right side of a fence because I'd gone north."
   188	
   189	"Yes."
   190	
   191	She made no reply to that, and he had not looked for one. She had told six people in a loft that at any edge somebody ought to be standing on the right side with her sleeves pushed up; and he watched her understand, with her eyes on a ring of pencil, that five of them had taken her at her word on a hillside two days from anywhere, and had turned round. When she bent to the fourth ring again, she had lost her place on it.
   192	
   193	"Then nobody knows what it stops," she said. "Good. Keep it so till somebody does."
   194	
   195	He had one more thing for her, which was not on the hide. He had carried it for ninety-eight days, and nobody had asked him to bring it over a river. But he was standing at the plank where he had once lain still and let her spend herself on him, and he could not stand there and keep a thing back.
   196	
   197	"There's another. It isn't a body's, and I don't know that it's yours. But you've had everything else."
   198	
   199	He gave it to her in order. There was a surveyor's cord, knotted at the fives. Four people had measured the inn yard with it, and had forty-two strides; he had measured it three times and had a piece under forty-two each time. There were two strips of brass, which were lying under her eyes. In his hand, against marks pricked in a table, one of them had come up a hair short and the other had gone a hair long, and in every other hand they were true. He had not touched any of the three since, and he had ruled *hold*.
   200	
   201	She heard the whole of it standing. Once she glanced at his hands, which were on the edge of her plank.
   202	
   203	"I don't know what that is," said Oryn. She pushed her sleeves up, from habit, and looked at what she had done, and pulled them down again. "Neither do you. Write that down too."
   204	
   205	"It's written."
   206	
   207	"Then you've done what there is to do." She took her eyes off his hands. "I wrote that you were to lie still when I said. I've not said. I spent this morning on a foot, and I've a bench tomorrow, and I've had a river in the dark off you seven times already. When I put my hands on that again I'll know what I'm reading for, and I'll have slept. Not here. Not yet."
   208	
   209	"I wasn't asking."
   210	
   211	"I know that you weren't. It's why I'm telling you."
   212	
   213	She went to the sill, where her strip of canvas lay, and began to roll it.
   214	
   215	"I'm on the road in the morning. Lowmarch is my first stop after a thaw, and the mill's my second; they have been for thirty rounds, and I'd be walking down to that ferry tomorrow if I'd never set eyes on the five of you." She did not turn round. "So I'll be on your road for two days, since it's mine as well. I'd have that understood. Nobody asked me."
   216	
   217	"Nobody did," said Brom, from the door. He was already counting bowls on his fingers.
   218	
   219	---
   220	
   221	They were six at the lower inn's hearth on the night of the two hundred and sixty-eighth, with a hundred and eighteen marks in the purse.
   222	
   223	The purse had moved by two at the landing. The ferryman had been on his rope-house roof when they walked down the bank, on his knees, with his palm laid on Brom's shingles. It had kept a thaw out, and he said so, and five marks came up through Pike's hand before the supper was on, with a line said twice to go with them. Then he had looked at the brown mule, who was standing at his shed's door as though she had never left it; and he had asked at last. It was three, for the cold term, straw and standing, and he had named it without once lifting his eyes from her.
   224	
   225	"A hundred and sixteen. Five on. Three off," said Brom. "A hundred and eighteen in hand."
   226	
   227	"I told you that he would," said Pike.
   228	
   229	Brom laid six bowls. He looked along the row, and did not take one back. But Oryn would not sit on the bench with the five; she stood at the board's end with her bowl in her hand, a stride and a half from the nearest of them, where she could see the door.
   230	
   231	Cael counted the board, as he counted any room. There was a big man with one arm bound and six bowls laid; and a girl who kept turning her left hand over on the wood, as though it had come back to her from a mender's; and a small pale woman with a hide; and a quiet one with his back to the wall; and at the end, on her feet, a healer who had walked two days down a saddle because it was her road.
   232	
   233	Karis waited until the bowls were empty. She had the hide on the board, spread upon Seln's oilcloth, with the brass laid along its pencil line, and a leaf beside it with eleven lines.
   234	
   235	"I am going to make a case," she said. "I have made it to one of you already, so that he should not hear it first in company. I shall say what I think, and what it rests on, and what it does not rest on; and I shall ask for nothing tonight."
   236	
   237	She put her finger on the first ring.
   238	
   239	"These are cut with marks. I have fifty-two sheets of them. They are the same forty-one kinds on three floors, and they stand in the same order on all three. People do not copy a tale so, or a list of the dead. Between the courses there is a line that carries the measure of the circle it stands in." She drew breath. "I think that they are instructions. A thing that has its own size written on its wall is telling somebody how it is made. I cannot read one word, and I know what kind of writing it is, as any of you would know a bill from a letter across a room."
   240	
   241	"Instructions for what?" said Lira.
   242	
   243	"I do not know. I should be guessing, and I have not come this far to guess."
   244	
   245	She moved the finger to the kit's ledger, which lay shut at her elbow.
   246	
   247	"Second. Somebody has classed these places. There are four signs at the head of that book, out of an index that none of us can read, and a mark beneath them that stands on two of the four. A clerk did not work that out in a hole. He looked it up. So there is a key, and the key is kept."
   248	
   249	"Where?" said Brom.
   250	
   251	Karis put her palm on the oilcloth where the bearing left the hide.
   252	
   253	Seln watched the fire as if he knew the road she meant.
   254	
   255	Karis answered him without looking away from the map.
   256	
   257	"It's west," she said. "Treswick. The last Compact city on the Line. There's a registry office on the old town, and under the old town there's an archive that was there before the Compact was — the Deepstacks, the scholars call it, when they call it anything — records nobody converted because nobody was ordered to and nobody needed to. Whatever these are" — her hand on the oilcloth — "if there's a word for them anywhere on the continent, it's in a book under that registry that nobody has opened since before there was a registry to open it. And there's a provision. The old scholars' provision. It lets a registered historical-studies practitioner in. I'm not one, out here. There's a man there who is."
   258	
   259	"It has a gate, with a book in it," said Seln. He had not taken his eyes from the fire. "And a station on its crown. There is a paper of ours that executes when an enrolled name is written in a book inside a district; it must be served within the hour. I told you so on the ninth day, and nothing has changed it. And there is one among us whose file is not shut."
   260	
   261	Cael felt the whole board take care not to look at him.
   262	
   263	It was Oryn whom he was watching. At the word *station* her head had come round, and her mouth had opened; he saw the sentence arrive in it, whole, as he had seen her sentences arrive since a mountain. She shut her mouth on it. She looked into her bowl, and turned the bowl a little in her fingers, and said nothing at all.
   264	
   265	He had never known that woman to swallow a thing which she thought true, and he had nowhere to put the sight of it.
   266	
   267	"That's back up the ladder," said Lira. Her left hand lay on the board, open, where she could look at it. "I've a certificate in this coat. Iron, Rank One, with a seal on it. I'd almost forgotten which pocket."
   268	
   269	"What does it cost?" said Brom.
   270	
   271	"All of you, something," said Karis. "Him, more than the rest of us together. I have not pretended otherwise to myself, and I shall not to you."
   272	
   273	"A registry's where they file you," said Cael.
   274	
   275	It was all that he had. He had kept out of such places since his Kindling, by every road that there was; and the one person at that board who had never asked him for anything was asking him to walk into the oldest of them, under a station, with a hide under her arm.
   276	
   277	"I'm not ruling tonight," he said. "Nothing leaves this bank till the ice goes, and the ferryman says that's a week. I'll hear it again in daylight, when I've slept on it. All of it, with the six."
   278	
   279	"That is what I asked for," said Karis, and folded her leaf.
   280	
   281	Brom gathered six bowls, and the fire ticked, and Cael sat on with her first two words going round in him. They were small words. Every road that the five had walked since the Line led away from them; and not one of the six had ever said the second of the two aloud as a place to go.
   282	
   283	---
   284	
   285	He took a lit lantern down to the ferryman's shed at dusk on the two hundred and seventieth day, and he carried it by the chain.
   286	
   287	He had begun a cold term in that doorway, with a mule and a frozen trough. The mule was there still, a hundred and thirty-nine days on, with her fee paid. The trough was open water. He went out to the end of the planks before he sat down, and gave the read the ground, as he had on the first morning; and he could feel the shelf going. It was grey, and soft, and full of water, and it took his weight on one side and let it drain away on the other. Out in the channel the river ran black from bank to bank between two rims of rotten ice.
   288	
   289	Every count that he kept had stopped at that water, and they were all about to move again. There was a second hazel wand under Seln's arm with forty notches in it. There were eleven lines in a one-armed man's book. There was a girl up at the inn who had lifted a bench with two hands that afternoon, for no reason, and put it down, and lifted it again.
   290	
   291	He sat on the mounting block with Hesk's book on his knee. He had filled ten lines in that shed, one to a fragment, and had thought it a whole cold term's work; he saw now that it had only been the part that he could do by himself. He wrote the rest, since there was nobody to hear him say it.
   292	
   293	*Two hundred and seventieth day.*
   294	
   295	*The ferry runs when the ice goes. Karis has a bearing, an index she can't read, and a language she can't read, and she says the answers are in a border archive nobody's opened since before the Compact. Since my Kindling I've stayed out of registries because a registry is where they file you. She wants me to walk into the oldest one on the continent. The kit reads everyone but me. So does the world, mostly. Nineteen. Ice goes next week. West, and inside, for the first time since the academy above Ostrand — which is a sentence I did not think I'd write again.*
   296	
   297	He read the last of it over. He did not like it, and it was true; he kept the pencil's point on the leaf beneath it for a long breath and made no mark.
   298	
   299	Outside the hurdles, in the dark, somebody had begun to knock the blocks out from under a scow.

