   239	Teague weighed that for as long as he would have given a slip.
   240	
   241	"I never put a day in my term," he said. "There's a block on the second ledge of the cold road that wants shifting, and salt behind it. If you're gone when I come down, leave it with him which way."
   242	
   243	"I'll leave it with Pike."
   244	
   245	The chin came up by the width of a finger. Then he went back up the bank to his four, and the boy, who had been watching the whole of it over a half-tied pack, bent to his knots again.
   246	
   247	---
   248	
   249	The reeds came down the river on the same flood that was holding up the boat, and the ferryman's boy had fished out two armfuls with a rake.
   250	
   251	They wove the hurdle that evening on the shed's floor, by the lantern, which hung from its nail by the chain. Cael had driven nine hazel rods into the beaten earth of the horse-walk in a row, a hand apart, to be its bones. Lira sat on her heels at one end and he at the other, and they passed the reeds in and out between the rods, turn about, each of them beating a finished course down with the edge of a hand before the next was begun.
   252	
   253	The old hurdle leaned against the drum, with a hole through the middle of it as wide as a fist, and clean edges.
   254	
   255	"That's my hurdle," said the ferryman, from his keg in the doorway. He had a block across his knees.
   256	
   257	"It's your next one," said Lira. "That's your last."
   258	
   259	"I can see the difference. One's got the night coming through it."
   260	
   261	"I owe you a hurdle," said Cael. "I'd sooner pay it than have it named."
   262	
   263	The ferryman grunted, and bent to his sheave. Behind him the dusk came down on the landing, and with it the quietest of the ten lifted in Cael to the height of his knees, as it did at every dusk, and was held there while he went on weaving.
   264	
   265	It was two-handed work, and she had known that when she sat down to it. A reed had to be bent round the end rod and held hard while the next was brought through, or the whole course sprang. She held with the left. He saw her set that hand on the first reed as she would have set it on something hot; the fingers closed, slowly, each a little after the one before, and the arm above them, which was still no thicker than a rail, began to shake.
   266	
   267	"It's got it," he said.
   268	
   269	"I know that it's got it. I'm watching it." She brought the next reed through with her right, and beat down, and shifted her grip. "It's like sending a boy on an errand. He goes. He comes back with the right thing. But you could have had your supper while you waited."
   270	
   271	"A month, she said."
   272	
   273	"She said slow for a month, and this is the seventh day. I've counted." They wove four courses, while the lantern swung a little on its chain, and the mule shifted in her straw beyond the drum. "I showed that paper yesterday as if it were a tooth I'd had drawn. Did you see?"
   274	
   275	"I saw."
   276	
   277	"I got it standing on a plate with half a hall looking. It was the best morning that I'd had. And he says that a civil man at a gate keeps it and gives me a slip." She beat a course down harder than it needed. "I'm not asking what you'll rule. I'm saying that I sat there and tried to mind, and I couldn't tell whether I minded. I've not stood on a floor for two hundred and seventy days. I don't know any longer what I'd be holding it for."
   278	
   279	He had no answer that was better than hers, and he did not make one up; he passed her a reed.
   280	
   281	"What I do know is this." She had stopped weaving. She took her left hand off the work and put it into her coat, and brought it out again, and looked at it, as she did a dozen times a day to see that it still came when it was called. "I've had one arm since the Fallow. I learned to do everything with one, and I got good at it. I can lead a mule round a drum with one, and coach a boy. Nobody's asked that arm to hit anything, or stop anything, since the day it was torn. She said to use it."
   282	
   283	"She said not to fall on it."
   284	
   285	"That's the second half of the order. I've been keeping the second half for seven days, and it's very easy; I'm excellent at it. Tomorrow I begin on the first." She bent a reed round the end rod with her left hand, and held it there, shaking, until he had brought the next one through. "The square, at first light. Wood. Three touches. And you're not to walk round my left side as if there were a pond in it."
   286	
   287	"There's that rule about what a healer's had her hands on."
   288	
   289	"She's had her hands on all of me. You'd have nothing left to hit." Lira beat the course down. "Both hands," she said, to the hurdle; and the ferryman, in the doorway, turned his block over and began on its other cheek.

