editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:173:Cael was more afraid on that river than he had been in a hall with a thing under its floor. He had had ten fragments to choose among there, and nobody to see which he chose. Here he had one, and its whole reach was the size of a table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:93:Cael saw it then, and he saw that she had had it first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:181:She had never in her life wanted an object as she wanted that page. It was not only that it would save her a year. She had been right before, in an annex, with three leaves on a trestle before it was light; she had been right, and it had made no difference, since she had had nothing to show a seat but her own rubbings and her own word. Stone proved that marks existed. It took a text to say what they meant, and it took a text that somebody else had kept to make anybody believe her.
