editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:257:716:"It's west," she said. "Treswick. The last Compact city on the Line. There's a registry office on the old town, and under the old town there's an archive that was there before the Compact was — the Deepstacks, the scholars call it, when they call it anything — records nobody converted because nobody was ordered to and nobody needed to. Whatever these are" — her hand on the oilcloth — "if there's a word for them anywhere on the continent, it's in a book under that registry that nobody has opened since before there was a registry to open it. And there's a provision. The old scholars' provision. It lets a registered historical-studies practitioner in. I'm not one, out here. There's a man there who is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:85:716:"Under the book." Seln put one finger on the wood beside the notebook. "Every counter in every district keeps a list, and the man reads each name against it as he writes, as you would look down a tally. Most days there is nothing. But there is a paper with four names on it, which has lain in a drawer since the close of a year, since it has found nothing yet to take hold of. It is not angry; it has only been waiting for a counter to be served at, and a book for somebody to be written in. It executes when one of those four names is written in a book inside a district, and the code says that it must be served within the hour. The code is obeyed, for the man who writes the name is the man who serves the paper."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:25:724:Cael turned the four sentences over while the stick came across the floor, since he had never heard that man waste one. A person who arrived in this country with a seal came over the board's head: he sent a clerk to the plank, or a paper, and had himself posted. This one had been told three times to go to twelve feet of oak, and had gone, and had read it from the left-hand post as any carter's boy would have; he had been asked what he had done, and had answered for himself, and had been taken at his word. He had paid a copper and a half for a loaf that cost the meadow a copper. The board had done to him what it did to everybody. Seln had wanted to know, before he stood away from a door, whether the man had noticed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:213:763:"Then you will understand me the more readily when I say that my trail stops there. I could not follow it. An archive on the Line keeps a book at its door, as a gate does, and every reader is written in it with the office that he comes from. My name stood in such books for forty years as the certifying office. If I had gone to that door with the seal, the request would have been minuted, and whatever shelf I had asked for would have been held by evening. If I went without it, I should be an old man of no standing, asking for the lowest shelf of a border archive, and I should not be let past the porter." The left hand opened a little, and shut. "I have never been able to go where that line points. I have only been able to carry it to somebody who might."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:123:702:"I do not know. I held it to a window, and to a candle, and it has been laid on both sides." Vastin put one raw finger beside the bar, as Brom had put his beside the page. "This is the one thing on that sheet which I can read, and I had better tell you why. A clerk who withdraws a word strikes it once, with a pen, so that it can still be read beneath the stroke; the code requires that, and I taught it to every junior that I had. That is not a clerk's stroke. It is a brush. I have seen it on perhaps a dozen papers in forty years, and I never once saw initials beside it. It is laid on by somebody who has the authority to make a thing unsaid, and who does not care to be known for having used it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:59:771:"It's the same road, and I can read. I'm not going to stand at this post and pretend that I took a job without seeing where it lay, and I'd not ask any of you to pretend it either." He made himself look at them then: a big man with four yards of webbing on him, and a girl with her left hand in her coat pocket, and a small pale woman holding six years of somebody's counting under her arm. "The boxes come. So do the papers, and the kit. I'll not leave a thing of ours in a store for a week with a river between. When the cut's done we'll be two days nearer than we are this morning, with the road going on in front of us, and I'll know more about what I'm walking with than I do now. That's all that I can honestly say I've decided. It's a job. I'll not make it a vow."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:101:900:Everything that he owned came to him then at once, and each of the ten had its reason. Edge? said the ninth, of the steel in his fist, and it would have gone through that grey neck like a wire through cheese. Pressure had been gathering since the first hound came over the bank. It lay under his breastbone as tight as a held breath, narrow and deep, and he had learned in a shed what it did afterward. It would go through the hound, and on, into a man's knee. There? asked Anchor, of the place where the plane met the scree, in its thin note. A point held there would have shut the bank like a gate, and it would have taken every other thing that he had while he held it. Storm showed him the air of the cut as a lane already half laid, from the head to the foot, and what a lane would do to six hounds; and after it he would be ten counts late at everything, in the one place where a beat mattered.

