editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:225:"A hundred and sixteen. Five on. Three off," said Brom. "A hundred and eighteen in hand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:19:Lira could not drive a peg, and Seln drove it for her. She ran the cord out with her right hand, and drew it, and set her pebbles with the toe of the great boot. She arrived at the scratch on the trough with two strides in hand. Brom did it left-handed, and came to forty-two. Seln did it without appearing to hurry and without any waste, as he did everything, and had the same.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:123:She had done this once before, on the hundred and sixteenth evening, at a fire, on the day after that cord came up out of the ground. She had the figure in her trials leaf. She did not look at it until she had her new one written down, since a person who knows what she is hoping to find will find it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:199:He laid the lane on the hundred and seventy-sixth night, in the meadow below the ash, when the three fires were covered. Storm was the seventh of the ten, a Gold's worth of weather that had come to him out of a bout with a Gold, and it was the heaviest thing that he carried. It wanted the air. To give it the air he had to take the ground from the read, since at that weight he could have one or the other. It was like shutting an eye.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:215:*That is the ten. Deployment since the Line: twenty-nine.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:243:*Deployment since the Line: twenty-nine.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:159:"I've no doubt of it. He asked for four." Brom let the end go. "A four's laid round a heart, and it keeps its round in a block, and a ferry's rope lives in blocks. You could give me this, and I'd still be owing him sixty yards of four." He put the purse inside his shirt, where the dead slips lay. "A man lent us a rope and told us what he wanted back. I'll not hand him something else and call it paid, however good it is. When you've four, there's a hundred and eighteen marks will come down this road to look at it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:259:*Two hundred and seventy-eighth day. A halting-ground on the border road, eleven miles from the river. A slip taken at the plank: a clearance, the cut below the shoulder hold, twenty. Five hands. Pike has a sixth line on it for the healer, of her own road. Vastin walks behind us. He was not asked, and he was not told no. He has the four hands, and he knows where a post stands. He says that he cannot promise to watch one of us bitten, and I believe him. The rope on the far bank was three-strand; Brom would not have it. In hand, a hundred and eighteen.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:263:*Deployment since the Line: thirty.*

