editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:53:4:It was the leaf that she had made on a mountain, and all five of them knew what was on it. A shale-back did not answer fire, or noise, or a blow struck on its own rock. It answered one thing: fright in another of its kind, which the whole herd felt in the same instant, and fled from. By that, and by what the carters said, it would answer a declaration in the same fashion. She had written *derived* beneath it, and *not tested*, and sixty-five days had not altered either word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:113:4:"It comes out and it goes home. Did you see? Out's quick. You'll not beat *out*; nobody will, off that one. But it has to be gathered again before it can come a second time, and that's slower, because there's seven feet of it to gather." She slapped her knee twice, a quick one and a slow one. "Out. Home. You go on *home*. Not back, and not round. In. Down the outside of the wood, close enough to rub your sleeve on it, till you're past the front hand. Inside the hands a pole's a broom."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:129:4:He walked into a shovel's haft for a quarter of an hour, in front of a bench and four pegs and whatever of the meadow had not gone back to its fires, and he was told *late* eleven times and *early* three before he had one that she did not name. She counted him as a man at a rail had once counted her, on a bluff, every morning for a month, and she was no kinder than that man had been; her right hand went on slapping the two beats long after his own legs had begun to shake.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:37:4:The second time he did not wait to be sure. The rag came straight for his breastbone, and he let it have the place where he had been, by a hand's breadth, so that seven feet of ash went by his left shoulder close enough to stir his hair. *Out.* He felt it stop at the end of two long arms. *Home.* It began to gather; and he went in down the outside of the wood, with his sleeve rubbing along it, one stride and a second, until the leading hand was behind his elbow and the whole pole was a thing that lay beside him and pointed at nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:49:4:The tall one's hands had moved. They had been at the pole's butt and its first third; now they lay a yard apart about its middle, so that there were three and a half feet of wood before the leading hand and three and a half behind. It came out, and it was not long enough to reach him. He saw that. He went in on *home*, as he had been taught, and *home* was over before he had taken his first stride, since there was only half as much to gather. The other end of the pole came round from behind its owner's hip and met him under the short ribs on the right.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:191:4:He could see almost nothing by then. The snow a foot from his face was a white blur; he could not have counted his own fingers. He had never held the read for half so long, or leaned on it so hard, and it had taken his near sight from him piece by piece as the price. He went on backward on his elbows with his eyes shut, since they were of no use open, and called the ground to a man whom he could hear and could not see. *Left. Straight. Stand. Walk.* The boy sent each word up the bank, and up there a girl with one arm walked a mule round a drum and stopped her dead at every *stand*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:135:4:She had not made it for brass. She had made it as a girl, in a hall with a counter in it, on the day when a registrar laid a card before her that said *Iron*, and a numeral, and *certified*. The girl beside her had wept at hers. Karis had read her own twice, and had asked what instrument it had been taken with, and how that instrument was kept true; and the registrar had looked at her as at a child who inquires what the sky is fastened to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:25:4:*Out,* he thought, when the right came. *Home.* The left started. He did not go back from it. He went in down the outside of the wood, with his sleeve brushing along it, while the stroke was still on its way to the place where he had been; and he laid the end of his stave against her hip.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:155:4:"Not *will not*. *Cannot.* There is no key."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:75:4:"It is a title. It is not a name. A name is written as a name is; it does not take the article before it. A clerk writes *the registrar*, and *the keeper*, and he writes so of an office that somebody holds, and he does not trouble to say who held it." She drew breath. "I have read the Compact's paper from this year's forms back to the oldest charter that a certain house will let a person see. I have dated it by its stocks and by its seals, and I know four strata of it by the feel of their paper. I have never met that word in any stratum: not as a title, or as a trade, or as a thing that anybody was ever called."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:187:8:He showed them as they walked. A flat hand, with the palm to the ground, was *stand*: wherever a person was, and as he was. A shut fist was *seen*, and one looked where the fist went and did not ask. Two fingers lifted were *come on*; that was Seln's, mostly. And a hand turned over empty, as a girl had once turned hers over to him on a hillside, was *nothing*: the thing had gone, or it had never been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:101:4:Everything that he owned came to him then at once, and each of the ten had its reason. *Edge?* said the ninth, of the steel in his fist, and it would have gone through that grey neck like a wire through cheese. Pressure had been gathering since the first hound came over the bank. It lay under his breastbone as tight as a held breath, narrow and deep, and he had learned in a shed what it did afterward. It would go through the hound, and on, into a man's knee. *There?* asked Anchor, of the place where the plane met the scree, in its thin note. A point held there would have shut the bank like a gate, and it would have taken every other thing that he had while he held it. Storm showed him the air of the cut as a lane already half laid, from the head to the foot, and what a lane would do to six hounds; and after it he would be ten counts late at everything, in the one place where a beat mattered.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:141:4:*Away*, he thought. *Not at.*
