editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/completion/LANE-A-REPORT.md:192:Formula metrics (`formula_metrics.py`, one chapter at a time): every chapter that was inside a working range before is inside it after. Ch13, ch15 and ch18 were already under 2.5% on the forty-word share and are unchanged. No sentences were joined.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/completion/LANE-A-REPORT.md:230:2. **Protected-line list.** `laneA/protected_lines.py` lists every manuscript line that shares a run of six or more words with BOOK_MAP §10, matches a line of `protected-patterns.txt`, or is an italic/document line (145 lines in ch1–30). After the pass the same 145 lines are present with identical text.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/completion/LANE-A-REPORT.md:231:3. **Hunk check.** `laneA/check_hunks.py` tests each hunk against that list. No hunk is on a protected line. Two hunks are within one paragraph of one, and both are narration only: ch4:261 (the paragraph after a log line) and ch21:231 (the paragraph before the protected log). See §7.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/completion/LANE-A-REPORT.md:233:5. **Beside-exchange rule.** Short protected lines that an n-gram cannot catch ("Four hands," / "Four." / "Right.", "Off floors,", "Noted", "I looked at it.") were handled by hand while reading: I changed nothing in or next to those exchanges, including their attributions. Six narration tags inside ordinary (unprotected) dialogue paragraphs were changed (ch13:201, ch14:47, ch18:157, ch20:279, ch23:309, ch28:135); the speech in each is untouched.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/completion/LANE-A-REPORT.md:237:- **Looking-away.** One change only (ch15:173). In these chapters the rest is the station promise (Cael and Lira from ch19 on), the gate mechanism (keepers and clerks who look at feet and figures, not faces), Cleon's habit, or a tag between two halves of one speech, where cutting it would have joined the speeches.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/completion/LANE-A-REPORT.md:239:- **Silence tags beside protected lines** (ch5:109, ch9:185, ch16:163, ch18:229, ch19:97, ch30:275) and the epithets listed under Priority 1: left because of where they stand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/completion/LANE-A-REPORT.md:241:- **ch12:85**, the fifth use of "a small pale woman had laid her palm on a strip of oilcloth": the formula is thinned in ch7, ch10 and ch13 and left here, inside the page exchange.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/completion/LANE-A-REPORT.md:246:2. **Two hunks next to logs.** ch21:231 ("and he found that it had been easier to say" → "and it had been easier to say") is in the paragraph that leads into the protected ch21 log; ch4:261 ("a quiet man" → "Seln") is in the paragraph after a log line. Both are narration and both are outside the units. Say if either should go back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/completion/LANE-A-REPORT.md:247:3. **Names and the skeleton probe.** Putting a name where an epithet stood moves a sentence toward the source's vocabulary. Two such edits crossed the probe's 0.35 line and I undid them (ch18:117, ch22:211). Smaller upward moves remain inside sentences that do not change class (largest: ch28:283 0.15 → 0.24; ch10:201 0.22 → 0.28; ch15:197 0.40 → 0.45, already close before). No chapter count rose. Lane B is making twice as many name substitutions; `laneA/close_pairs.py` gives the before/after ratio for every changed sentence without printing source text, if you want the same test run there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/state/completion/LANE-A-REPORT.md:251:7. **ch4:261.** I confirmed against the Book 7 edition (`book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-52.md:359`, "Seln's keeping the ledger") that the "quiet man" who gave Karis the surveyor's book is Seln. No name was supplied for any unnamed role.
