editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:287:"I knew," said Lira. "That's what you did to mine, on the Fallow. I've been sawing bread with it ever since."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:125:"What they say. I have fifty-two sheets, and forty-one kinds of mark, and I can tell you the order in which those kinds stand on three floors. I cannot tell you what a single one of them means." She squinted along a strip from the Fallow. "I have never learned to read a thing from the thing alone. Nobody has. There must be something to lay beside it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:75:It went up slowly, and a little crooked at the elbow, and it shook. But it was the first time since a white floor on the Fallow that she had voted with it, and she held it there until he had seen. Brom's left went up beside it. Karis shifted the flat, and raised her right hand with the glove still on. Seln lifted two fingers, and Cael put up his own.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:141:Cael wrote his letter that afternoon, at the meal-sack end of the deal. He had owed it since a wash on the Stair road, to a tall exact person on a bluff who had written that he still lost every time he ran it. For the whole of a cold term he had not known what to say to a friend who was walking a bout through by himself in an empty yard. He knew now, since a girl with grass in her hair had shown him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:281:"What I do know is this." She had stopped weaving. She took her left hand off the work and put it into her coat, and brought it out again, and looked at it, as she did a dozen times a day to see that it still came when it was called. "I've had one arm since the Fallow. I learned to do everything with one, and I got good at it. I can lead a mule round a drum with one, and coach a boy. Nobody's asked that arm to hit anything, or stop anything, since the day it was torn. She said to use it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:11:It was a broker's paper, with a hook pressed in dark wax at its foot and a bar under the hook. Lira read its head upside down from the meal-sacks, with her lips, and Cael read it with her. *Fallow Ring: crew returned, instruments not recovered.* Under that stood a town on no registry map, and a board; and where a crew's name should have been there were five strokes, and after the strokes a count, which was six.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:65:*—of the Architect: see the lower archive at Treswick, which holds it in full—*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:101:Lira looked at the healer for the length of a breath. Then she bent her head, and got the knot between her teeth, and worked at it with her right hand until it gave. The grey wool slid down. She drew it off over her head; and the arm that had lain against her breast since a white floor on the Fallow, through a stair and a hall and the whole of a cold term, came down by its own weight and hung at her side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:167:It was dry. She set a pebble on each corner, and laid the older strip of brass along the pencil line; and Cael laid Hesk's beyond it, so that the two made one bright edge from the ring by the Fallow to the bare skin past the fourth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:257:"It's west," she said. "Treswick. The last Compact city on the Line. There's a registry office on the old town, and under the old town there's an archive that was there before the Compact was — the Deepstacks, the scholars call it, when they call it anything — records nobody converted because nobody was ordered to and nobody needed to. Whatever these are" — her hand on the oilcloth — "if there's a word for them anywhere on the continent, it's in a book under that registry that nobody has opened since before there was a registry to open it. And there's a provision. The old scholars' provision. It lets a registered historical-studies practitioner in. I'm not one, out here. There's a man there who is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:93:There were five people in a room that had been built for sacks, and the only sound in it was the fire on the other side of the wall. Cael looked at two strips of brass. One had come from Ternhall in a researcher's coat, and one from Denvash in a bag, and he had laid both of them along a pencil line at a hearth thirty-seven days ago and found them true. He felt the cold of that yard in his stomach.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:171:She knew that mark from four places. It was struck into the head of the stake in that case, and into the newer of two iron bars at a grey stone on the Stair road; it was on every bar of a fence round a place that she had not been let into, under the number thirty-four; and it was here. It stood beside the Stair twice over and beside the fourth place, and she had never found it beside the Fallow or the hall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:173:She had turned that over until it was as smooth as a river stone. Two had the mark, and two had not; and the two that had it were neither the two largest, since the Stair was of one size with the others, nor the two nearest to anything. A girl with her arm in a sling had pointed out, on a morning by a marsh, which way the numbers ran, and Karis had entered the order and declined to say what was being ordered. She declined still. She had been at that mark for fifty-five days, and she could not have said what it counted, or whether it counted anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:177:It was this that she could not let go, and she knew it for what it was. She had spent the whole of her working life drawing answers out of stone a stroke at a time, by argument, against people who did not wish to be argued with. Every one of those answers was hers, and every one was only as strong as her reasoning. Here was a thing of another kind. A clerk had sat at a desk, with a book open, and had looked up what sort of place the Long Stair was, and had found it. He had written it down in four signs and two strokes of a punch. He had not derived it. He had been told.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:225:"I know when it stopped." Brom held the loaf down on the board for her, and she cut it in five, one-handed, and the crust cracked under the steel. The room smelled of barley, and of the smith's oil. "That's the first bread in this inn since the Fallow that hasn't been torn."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:195:"To an archive. The lower one, at a town on the Line." He said the name as a man reads out a place from a waybill, with no notion of what it would do. "Treswick."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:65:"It's why I asked you. A hundred and sixty-three days ago six people stood on a rim, over a place called the Stair, with crossbows. They'd cloths across their faces. No seals, no word, nothing you could have written down. One of them put a bolt in him. They knew which hole we were in before we'd climbed out of it." She leaned across the board. "You say nothing of yours crosses the Line. Something did."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:69:"That is not mine," he said. "I shall say it as you have, so that there is no room in it. The rim of the Stair is not mine. I never sent a man east of the Line in forty years, and I never knew that any office had. I learned that somebody had on the morning when a broker's report came to my desk with no routing note upon it." His eyes came back to her. "I do not know whose they were. I looked. You should know that I looked for the whole of a cold term, with every key that I still had, and found nothing that carried a name; and that it frightened me a good deal more than a name would have."

