editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:9:On the second afternoon the road left the river and climbed into a side valley, and at the head of the valley there was a gate with an axe-head nailed to its top bar.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:13:The man who came to the bar was short and thick, with a tally stick in his belt and a sheepskin over his shoulders. He was the hold's factor, and he gave them the look of a person counting sacks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:23:The factor thought about that, and it appeared to satisfy him, for he drew the bar. He did not ask what any of them was called, then or afterward. He asked whether they had brought a maul, and what they knew about stone wedges, and whether the big one could lift a nine-foot post left-handed; and when Brom had answered the last by doing it, in the yard, with a post off the stack, the man nodded and took them up the hill to see what he had.
