editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:65:It was registry iron, a foot and a half long. It had a number struck into its head, thirty-two, and beneath the number a short stroke and a bar. Its point had been turned on a wheel, and it was bright.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:11:It was a broker's paper, with a hook pressed in dark wax at its foot and a bar under the hook. Lira read its head upside down from the meal-sacks, with her lips, and Cael read it with her. *Fallow Ring: crew returned, instruments not recovered.* Under that stood a town on no registry map, and a board; and where a crew's name should have been there were five strokes, and after the strokes a count, which was six.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:119:Karis turned the sheet a little, and the lamp showed the stripe to the whole table. Somebody had drawn a brush across everything that followed that last stroke of the pen. The bar was as broad as a thumb and as black as a hearth-back, and it had been laid on so thickly that the ink had cracked in drying, like mud in a rut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:123:"I do not know. I held it to a window, and to a candle, and it has been laid on both sides." Vastin put one raw finger beside the bar, as Brom had put his beside the page. "This is the one thing on that sheet which I can read, and I had better tell you why. A clerk who withdraws a word strikes it once, with a pen, so that it can still be read beneath the stroke; the code requires that, and I taught it to every junior that I had. That is not a clerk's stroke. It is a brush. I have seen it on perhaps a dozen papers in forty years, and I never once saw initials beside it. It is laid on by somebody who has the authority to make a thing unsaid, and who does not care to be known for having used it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:127:"I do not know which event he meant," said Vastin. "The sheet does not say; and whatever would have said it is under that bar."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:133:She had her notebook open at a clean leaf already, and she copied the sheet as she had copied forty-one kinds of mark off three floors, a line for a line, with every stop where the technician had set it. At the date she wrote the office's own figures down exactly as they stood, and did not turn them into anything. When she came to the bar she ruled a box of the same length, and looked at it, and wrote two words within.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:153:Cael went back over it, as he would have gone back over a touch that he had not seen land. The others had all been looking at the bar. Seln had not. He had stood behind that stool with his eyes on her pencil; and when the pencil came to the figures he had stopped, hands and breath together, like a clock that somebody has laid a finger on, and had stayed so for exactly as long as it took her to write down a year and a day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:65:The gate had no axe-head on it, or anything else. It was a plain bar across a gap in a wall as thick as a cart, and the factor was at it before they had got their breath: a spare grey person in a leather apron, with a slate hung from the belt on a cord and a sheep-crook in one hand. A pair of pale eyes went along them and counted.
