editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:235:"He copied them from this," she said. "Or from the list that this was copied from. I told you that a clerk had looked it up." She did not sound pleased. She sounded like a person who has been right about where a wall is, in the dark, by walking into it. "It is the same index. I can read it no better than I could. But it is not a dead man's private mark any longer. It is an office's."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:247:Then she did a thing that was very like her. She went back through the whole row from its first sheet, quickly, looking at nothing but the heads; and each time that she met a group of four signs which she had not met before, she laid one finger of her left glove down upon the deal. She could not read a group. She could tell one from another, as a person with no letters can still tell his own mark from his neighbour's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:267:He paid at the gate, under the axe-head. He counted forty marks onto the top bar, and put a thumb-mark shaped like a wedge at the foot of Pike's slip.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:61:Most of it was a schedule. Cael could read it, since it lay the other way up to him: a column of housings, each with its number, and against each a line that gave the date of a setting and a mender's mark. It was a clerk's list, as dull as a tally of sacks, and a careful man had copied the whole of it down a page in order to show where one line fell.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:133:She had her notebook open at a clean leaf already, and she copied the sheet as she had copied forty-one kinds of mark off three floors, a line for a line, with every stop where the technician had set it. At the date she wrote the office's own figures down exactly as they stood, and did not turn them into anything. When she came to the bar she ruled a box of the same length, and looked at it, and wrote two words within.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:125:"What they say. I have fifty-two sheets, and forty-one kinds of mark, and I can tell you the order in which those kinds stand on three floors. I cannot tell you what a single one of them means." She squinted along a strip from the Fallow. "I have never learned to read a thing from the thing alone. Nobody has. There must be something to lay beside it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:171:"They go to where it was, and it's gone, and nothing's moving. So they stand. That's her fourth line. Five hounds, or more, standing on a stride of glass at the top of a hill with their heads swinging, and us below them, as quiet as posts." He touched the pebbles. "Brom here, twenty strides down from the mark, on the south edge, where there's a flat stone to stand on that isn't ice. Lira above him on the scree. Seln over the north bank, where they'll not see him and I shan't. When they come down to try us, they try Brom first. He's wrapped."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:197:"You'll have the foot of the cut," Cael said to Karis. "With the clock, and the mule, and the boxes. It's eighty strides from the mark and sixty from Brom, and it's the last place in that road that anything will come to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:217:Cael walked by the three carts. He had not meant to look at them, and he looked: a wheel with two spokes gone, and a barrel-head with a hold's mark burned in it, and under the nearest bed a mule's collar, with the straw coming out. Somebody had driven that cart for two days up a silent road with a Path in his pocket. He had kept it there until the very last hundred strides.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:237:He turned his back on the mark, and walked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:243:They came over the north bank at the mark, as Lira had said that they would, and he turned his head as far as a post may. There were five, grey and long, pouring over the worn places with their heads level. And behind those, out from under the strip that nobody had been able to see, there came three more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:249:They did not so much as check. The first of them set its feet on the glass at the mark, and turned its head down the hill, toward the foot, where three carts lay; and all eight of them came on.
