editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:7:They walked for two days up the north water under a sky like a pewter dish, and slept the night between in a hay barn that a carter had told Brom of, with the mule's breath going up among the rafters and Seln sitting out his watch in the doorway. Lira set the great boot down whole at every stride of both days. The bow rode on her back with its one bolt; nobody had proposed that she should stay by a hearth while a fence was mended, and nobody who knew her would have tried.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:17:"I set posts with the other one," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:157:It was the smallest calf that undid it. Its plates were paler than the rest, and it had kept to the inside of the line all day; but there was a trough on the fence line below, a hollowed log where the factor salted his cattle in the dry months, and a calf is a calf. It left the line. It came down the slope toward the salt at a wandering trot, with its beak out, and it set a forefoot on the thirty-eighth stone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:181:He was coming down from the strip with a fence rail on his left shoulder, twelve feet of split ash, with his Iron Skin unasked for and no grey on him anywhere. He went to the calf's upper side, between it and the herd. He set the rail's end under the animal's breast, behind the trapped leg, and laid the middle of it across the next cap-stone up the hill, and got his good shoulder beneath the long end.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:243:They set fourteen that day, and fifteen on the next, and twelve on the hundred and forty-third.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:251:Cael walked the whole fence on the last evening with Karis's notebook, to have the count right for Pike. There were forty-one new posts, with the earth dark about their feet. There were two more with fresh gravel round them, the twentieth and the twenty-first from the gate, in the middle of a run that another crew had set; and beside each, a hand to the north, a round scar showed in the snow where a post had lately stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:279:Pike said their line at dusk on the hundred and forty-sixth, in a room with thirty people in it and a fire that a man could have stood in. He gave it out as he gave everything, with his shoulder turned: a fence, at the axe-head hold, the long one; forty-one posts set and the far side pinned; a herd shifted off the line and nothing run; witnessed. He dipped his pen for the last column.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:9:Brom, who had six bowls laid along the board, set the ladle down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:35:By the next morning there was a square trodden in the snow under the great ash, twelve strides on a side, with a hazel peg at each corner. Somebody had carried a bench down from the upper inn and set it along the western edge. Lira took one end of it. Brom took the middle, since a shoulder in webbing was of no use in a square. At the other end, with her staff across her knees and a splinting board at her feet, sat a lean old woman in a cap, who looked at the knot on Brom's webbing as she sat down and at nothing else that belonged to the five.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:51:He had read this person a dozen times across rooms and roads, and never from so near. There was a long quiet face under a wool cap, with pale eyes in it, and a pair of big chapped hands. The feet were set a little apart in the snow, and nothing else about the tall one told him anything whatever.
