editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:215:It was the fourth night that he had seen it: two before he went up the cold road, and two since. He had not gone looking; the house on the rise had one window on that side, and a man who has kept a watch on roads for as long as he had does not sit with his back to a window. The light came on after dark and burned for an hour or two, and for the whole of that time a small still shape stood between two hurdles, facing outward. It was the quiet one. Teague had needed two looks to find him, once, in a room. He found him now in one, and took no credit for it, since the man was not trying.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:277:Brom had begun to laugh, silently, with his bound shoulder held still. "Twice. And nobody touched you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:3:The keeper would not have a mule under his eave for a whole cold term, and he said so at the inn's door, with his cloth over his shoulder, while five people were still looking at a healer's letter on a strip of bark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:27:It was the third of the ten, and Brom's, and the cheapest thing that he owned. It went out from the soles of his boots for a pace and a half and told him the mass of whatever lay within that distance, and which way the mass was going, and nothing more. It could have the ground or it could have the air, and never both. He gave it the ground. There were the planks, heavy and still on their piles. There was the shelf, one thick slab frozen fast to the bank, which sent its weight home into the gravel like a well-set stone. Past the shelf's edge he could only just find the skin. It weighed almost nothing, and it had nowhere to send even that; it lay on the water as a leaf lies.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:113:"You can count posts," said Brom. "I've a fence with forty of them still lying in a heap, by what he told Pike, and one arm to set them with. I'll want somebody on a rail who can see whether they stand in a row."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:175:"Mine doesn't stop. Mine's a run with the middle gone; I come down still running, and that's where the shin went, on a mountain. You don't run at all. You go, and then you're a post." She frowned at the place where he stood. "So it's no use for getting *at* anything. You'd arrive like a parcel, and whatever you'd come for would have a whole count to do as it liked with you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:295:He read the leaf over. It was a poor thing beside the inventory that he had made four nights ago, which had ten names in it, and a still place, and two promises. This had three fragments and the small plain facts of two evenings in a shed, and under them he had ruled seven lines and left them empty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:11:It had been nailed there for so long that the rust had run down the wood beneath it in a brown beard. Behind the gate a long house lay under a turf roof with smoke going straight up from it, and beside the house stood a byre that was longer still; Cael could hear cattle shifting in there from the road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:41:Oryn regarded him. She had her bowl in one hand still, and with the other she had begun, by old habit, to push a sleeve up; she looked down at that hand and stopped it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:43:"Then I still haven't asked," she said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:69:"That is not mine," he said. "I shall say it as you have, so that there is no room in it. The rim of the Stair is not mine. I never sent a man east of the Line in forty years, and I never knew that any office had. I learned that somebody had on the morning when a broker's report came to my desk with no routing note upon it." His eyes came back to her. "I do not know whose they were. I looked. You should know that I looked for the whole of a cold term, with every key that I still had, and found nothing that carried a name; and that it frightened me a good deal more than a name would have."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:97:It was not the day for the other question. That one was about a boy. This was about a thing that lay under plates, and the man who had carried it here had one answer still in him that somebody ought to draw out; and the person to draw it was the one with the pencil.

