editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:223:He thought of the cloth, which hung on its nail beside that same hearth. He could not read the sheet from here, since it was in another house, a hundred yards down a bank, in the dark; and he did not need to. His own name stood by itself on the first line, as it had for more cold terms than he cared to reckon, and ten lines under it there were five names in a row. It had said so at first snow, and it would say so, to the letter, until the first boat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:21:"I see whether they're in a row," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:25:The hillside rose behind the byre, steeply, for four hundred yards, to a band of grey rock that ran across it like the coping of a wall. The fence left the yard at the gate, and climbed that hill aslant, and then ran east along the foot of the rock. Teague's posts stood out of the snow the whole way up from the gate, six strides apart, as straight as a ruled line, for as far as he had got. Beyond the last of them a row of flat stones went on across the slope in the same line, each the size of a hearth-loaf, with a wisp of straw showing at its edge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:35:Cael had taken them for boulders. They lay along the eastern end of the row of stones, close in under the rock band, where no snow had drifted: a line of long grey backs, keeled, each as broad as a cart and roofed with plates that overlapped like slates. Snow lay in the seams between the plates. He had last seen such backs on a ledge over a bay, on the seventy-eighth day, a breath before a mountain came down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:113:They cleared it from the top side, as Lira had said, working east from a place twenty yards beyond the old cow, who lay last in the row. Seln and Cael had the keeper's shovels. Brom had lashed a barn board across the end of a pole, and he drove it before him left-handed, with the pole's butt in the pit of his good arm, so that it took off a yard of snow at a push. Karis came behind with a besom and swept what the shovels left. She had Hesk's clock on a stone, and a peg in the snow at every tenth yard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:127:They kept the strip forty yards ahead of her. It was hard labour, and it was the plainest work that Cael had done since the Line: a shovel, and a slope, and a row of pegs, and the sweat going cold between his shoulders whenever he straightened to look. Brom's board went up the hill at a hand in the yard, exactly, with no cord to guide it. He had laid that line with his eye, as he might have laid a course of stone, and the grey backs followed it upward as though it were a wall that they could not see over.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:113:"You can count posts," said Brom. "I've a fence with forty of them still lying in a heap, by what he told Pike, and one arm to set them with. I'll want somebody on a rail who can see whether they stand in a row."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:115:"I can see whether a thing's in a row."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:75:The factor regarded Vastin, who made the short bow, and it appeared to answer the purpose, since the bar was drawn. The yard within was small and very clean, with a long low house on one side and a salting-shed on the other. The shed's door stood open on a row of empty hooks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:251:They wove the hurdle that evening on the shed's floor, by the lantern, which hung from its nail by the chain. Cael had driven nine hazel rods into the beaten earth of the horse-walk in a row, a hand apart, to be its bones. Lira sat on her heels at one end and he at the other, and they passed the reeds in and out between the rods, turn about, each of them beating a finished course down with the edge of a hand before the next was begun.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:213:"Six rows," he said. "A year to a row, with the newest uppermost. They are abstracts. A surveyor in the field keeps his own book; at the year's end an office draws out of it what is to be kept, on a printed sheet, and that sheet is the abstract. These are six years of one survey."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:221:"Nothing is mine that I have not counted." She drew the gloves on, a finger at a time, and slipped the tape of the uppermost row, and turned back its first sheet by the edges.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:229:Karis went down the row with one gloved finger, lifting a corner at a time, until she came to a sheet whose number she knew; and then she reached behind her, without looking, to the tarred case, and brought out a limp little ledger, and laid it open beside the sheet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:237:There was one more column, at the right of every sheet, narrow, and ruled off from the rest. Its printed head was in plain letters, and it said what the office kept that column for: the anomaly. Karis went down it on the first sheet with one gloved finger. She turned to the second sheet, and the third; and then she lifted a corner in the lowest row of the six, which was the oldest, and looked there. It was the same on every sheet. In every clerk's hand, in every one of six years, that column held a single word, and nobody had ever written a second word beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:247:Then she did a thing that was very like her. She went back through the whole row from its first sheet, quickly, looking at nothing but the heads; and each time that she met a group of four signs which she had not met before, she laid one finger of her left glove down upon the deal. She could not read a group. She could tell one from another, as a person with no letters can still tell his own mark from his neighbour's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:259:"I shall not say a number tonight. I have turned one row of six, by a lamp, and I have counted signs that I cannot read by telling one group from another. I should not put my name to such a count." She lifted her hand from the deal. "I shall say how many when I have checked it in daylight."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:263:"There is one sheet more in that flat," said Vastin. "It lies under the sixth row, and it is not the office's. I should prefer that you had counted the rest before you saw it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:7:Vastin put his hand into the flat, beneath the sixth row, and drew out a single sheet that was tied with nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:107:"It is a technician's return," said Vastin. "When a man who services the stations finds a thing that he was not sent to find, he is bound to write it up on such a sheet, on the day, and send it in. The office had filed this one with the surveys, at the end of the second row, since it had no other shelf for it; and I should be glad if it went back there when you have done. I do not understand it. I have read it at every halt for three hundred miles, and I understand it no better than I did at the first."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:139:She went down her copy against the sheet, a word at a time, with the butt of her pencil. Then she did as she had been asked. She slipped the tape of the uppermost row and lifted that row out whole, and slid the technician's sheet in at the foot of the row beneath, and laid the first back upon it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:141:"The second row, at its end," she said. "I shall know where to find it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:173:*Received of Vastin, at Lowmarch, day two hundred and seventy-seven past the Line: one courier's flat, holding six rows of abstracts in their tapes, one row looked through and no sheet counted; one broker's report; one page, copied by him from a register; one technician's sheet, copied by me this night and laid back in the second row. Read in part. Understood in no part. — K.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:299:*Two hundred and seventy-seventh day, night. The first boat brought no paper for us. It brought one man, who was the Archmarshal and is not. He held the ferry on her cable with his back. He has handed over three things: six years of an office's surveys, which it told nobody; one line, copied from a register, that sends its reader to a town; and a sheet that somebody stopped with a brush. Karis has all three. She has looked through one row and copied one sheet, and she says that she understands none of it yet. The town is the one that she named. The price is what it was. I said that I would rule when the boat was in. Tomorrow, at the plank, with hands.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:5:He had crossed it a good many times. There had been a ledge of grey rock from bank to bank, ten feet broad, with the river sliding over it no deeper than a shin, and a row of white stones to show a wheel where the rock ended. There had been a bar of gravel in the middle that a person could stand on; he had fought on that bar, and had seen a hound drowned beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:103:It was as thin as a rail. It was white, and there were eight small marks along it in a row, as pale now as the skin about them. She lifted it, and it rose slowly, and shook, and the fingers opened and shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:229:Brom laid six bowls. He looked along the row, and did not take one back. But Oryn would not sit on the bench with the five; she stood at the board's end with her bowl in her hand, a stride and a half from the nearest of them, where she could see the door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:11:She folded the oilcloth over the page along its own creases, and did not look at him while she did it. The broker's sheet went under the sixth row, and the straps drew down across a red stamp which said that the thing was to go by hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:71:It had gone blue on the hundred and fifty-third night, as the ferryman had said that it would, with a groan that woke half the inn. He had walked it the next morning from bank to bank with an iron bar, sounding at every tenth stride, and had come home and cut spruce boughs. By noon there was a double row of them standing in the ice, a cart's width apart, going away up the north water as far as a person could see: his track. It swung wide at the bend above the landing, a furlong out of the straight, to keep clear of the place where the side water came in under the western bank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:269:"He begins with this. Four signs in a row, and after them the number, thirty-two. It is how he names the place that he is standing in." Her finger did not move. "They are a clerk's signs. They are not a carver's; I have forty-one kinds of mark from three floors, and these are not cut like any of them. Seln read registry paper for fifteen years, and he does not know the index that they are taken from."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:211:*Storm, from Daeva: one lane. Ten counts late; clean at eleven. The read goes blind to the ground while it lasts. Three in a row is as many as I have laid.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:189:It was full dark outside the hurdles by then, and the quietest of the ten fragments had come up with the dark, as it did at every dusk. Shadow lifted behind the rest, soft, as far as his knees. It was the only fragment that charged him for being shut, a little, from the minute he woke, like a lamp left burning in a room that nobody used. It could have made less of him. He could have stood in that lantern's light and not been there at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:103:"You had forty years' standing. You had a seal that every seat in the Compact was bound to receive, and the right to be heard by a convening on any finding that you chose to lay before it. There are councils that sit for exactly such a thing as this. You could have put three papers on a table under your own hand, and made them minute every line." She did not soften it. "You gave up the seal, and you carried them on your back to a board at a ferry. I am not asking why you left. I am asking why you did not use what you had before you left it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:61:"I have it from a minute. I prefer this."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:185:"I shall ask the next question, since somebody must, and I had sooner it were asked in a form that can be answered. I am not asking you who made it. I am asking whether any paper says how it came to be there: a register, or a founding minute, or anything else that an office would keep."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:121:He did not haul. It was that which held Cael's eye through the whole of what followed, with the read going dim at the edges and his own two hands beginning to blur. A younger man would have fought that rope. He would have pulled, and gained a foot, and lost it at the next surge, and worn out in a minute. The man in the brown coat pulled at nothing. He had made himself into the thing that the strap was fastened to. When the river took the stern, the turn on the post took most of the river; what was left came to his back and his heels, and he gave it nowhere to go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:255:It was Brom who saw the sacks. There was a boy of sixteen or so by the smith's rail, on the far side of the inn yard, trying to get two of them onto his shoulder where he had been given one; they were the long grey kind that meant salt, and a dozen more lay on the snow beside a pair of mules. A lean old woman in a cap stood by with a staff that she did not lean on. A tall figure held the mules' heads, with a spear grounded, and a broad man was easing his weight onto his right leg.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:35:By the next morning there was a square trodden in the snow under the great ash, twelve strides on a side, with a hazel peg at each corner. Somebody had carried a bench down from the upper inn and set it along the western edge. Lira took one end of it. Brom took the middle, since a shoulder in webbing was of no use in a square. At the other end, with her staff across her knees and a splinting board at her feet, sat a lean old woman in a cap, who looked at the knot on Brom's webbing as she sat down and at nothing else that belonged to the five.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:153:At the bench's far end a lean old woman got up, and looked at a knot, and went away up the bank without a word to anybody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:15:Cael had his own stave, which was no longer than his forearm and hand, with a rag bound round the grip. He stood four strides off. Wind was in his feet, as it was at the start of everything; it leaned him away to the right, with a burst ready behind the lean, and he told it no and felt his own two legs take the weight instead. The read asked next, civilly, for the ground under that stave. He shut it. No Paths meant the ten, whatever he called them, and he had written so. They were not much. He had bought them on this grass, a touch at a time, from a seven-foot pole.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:55:The bar of Thornwater's eastern gate was drawn for them before they had called, and on the platform beside it a lean man lifted two fingers from the stave of a bow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:99:Nobody in that yard moved, and on the platform the lean man had turned his back on the valley to watch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:127:It took the hour, which Brom counted by the shadow of the well's windlass on the flags. Karis sat on the coping with the roll of hide across her knees and did not once open her notebook, and Seln stood where he could see both gates. The lean man had come down off his platform, which Cael had never known him do, and waited a little way from the door with his stave grounded, looking at nothing. Cael watched the back of a healer who was spending, on a foot, what a child at some northern gate might want of her in a week; and he knew that she had weighed that before she pushed up her sleeves.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:161:She took the shirt out of the toe, a strip at a time. Then she put her mended foot into a boot that was two fingers too long for it, and stamped, and went across the yard to hear what a lean man on a platform had to say about a bow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:175:A lean old woman in a cap came down the bank from the house on the rise, a little before noon, with her staff under her arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:53:It was the leaf that she had made on a mountain, and all five of them knew what was on it. A shale-back did not answer fire, or noise, or a blow struck on its own rock. It answered one thing: fright in another of its kind, which the whole herd felt in the same instant, and fled from. By that, and by what the carters said, it would answer a declaration in the same fashion. She had written *derived* beneath it, and *not tested*, and sixty-five days had not altered either word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:27:It was the third of the ten, and Brom's, and the cheapest thing that he owned. It went out from the soles of his boots for a pace and a half and told him the mass of whatever lay within that distance, and which way the mass was going, and nothing more. It could have the ground or it could have the air, and never both. He gave it the ground. There were the planks, heavy and still on their piles. There was the shelf, one thick slab frozen fast to the bank, which sent its weight home into the gravel like a well-set stone. Past the shelf's edge he could only just find the skin. It weighed almost nothing, and it had nowhere to send even that; it lay on the water as a leaf lies.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:213:He opened the ledger at their heading and laid his stone on the leaf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:247:Pike wrote it, and said it over to the meadow, as he said everything. Forty fires had burned along that water in the dry months, with a cart or a tent to each. There were three now, and a great many black rings in the snow where the others had been; the hide-woman sat at the nearest with a skin across her knees, and she was the only person in half a mile of grass who lifted her head to hear what a crew had paid. Then his pen went down the leaf to the last line under their names, which was the season's, and stopped beside the ruled space at the end of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:283:He ruled a line under that, and began a new leaf. He headed it as he had never headed one before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:295:He read the leaf over. It was a poor thing beside the inventory that he had made four nights ago, which had ten names in it, and a still place, and two promises. This had three fragments and the small plain facts of two evenings in a shed, and under them he had ruled seven lines and left them empty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:307:"Then count something else," she said. "You've a leaf there with seven empty lines on it. I'd start with those."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:133:She had her notebook open at a clean leaf already, and she copied the sheet as she had copied forty-one kinds of mark off three floors, a line for a line, with every stop where the technician had set it. At the date she wrote the office's own figures down exactly as they stood, and did not turn them into anything. When she came to the bar she ruled a box of the same length, and looked at it, and wrote two words within.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:163:She had turned to a fresh leaf. Vastin, who had begun to draw the flat's straps together from mere habit, stopped with the buckle in his fingers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:167:"I do not give it because you ask. I give it because three things have changed hands in this room, and one of the hands was mine." She wrote steadily, in the small upright hand, and read it over once under her breath, and tore the leaf out along its fold. "You will find that it has no seal."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:155:Karis had her leaf of findings on the well's coping, with a stone on it, and the seven of them stood round it in the hold's yard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:87:Cael had watched her do it. Twice in that hour she had drawn breath, with her pencil lifted, and twice she had let the breath go and bent to the leaf again. She had a question. It was not a question about paper, or she would have asked it; she asked those as other people breathed. It was the other kind, which she thought ill-mannered, and she had been brought up in archives, where a person does not ask a keeper why he has kept a thing so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:183:Karis laid her pencil down across the leaf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:199:Nobody started, since they were not people who started. But Karis's right hand, which had been lying on her notebook, had gone flat upon it as if the leaf might blow away; Seln's eyes had left the yard, and Lira's fingers, on the board, had drawn in. Brom's two hands had stopped on the board. And at the board's end a healer who had swallowed a sentence nine days ago, at this same table, when a station was spoken of, stood with her bowl in both hands and looked at nobody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:7:The square was as Teague had left it, twelve strides on a side, with a hazel peg at each corner and the bench along its western edge. The snow had gone from it. What lay there now was last year's grass, flattened and brown, with a skin of frost on it that would be water by the middle of the forenoon. Brom sat on the bench to keep the count. At its far end the tall man had come down by himself, as he had on most mornings of the cold term, with his spear upright between his knees and its new leaf of steel in a leather cap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:217:They were making ready for a road. The tall man sat on the mounting block with seven feet of ash across his knees; he had drawn off the wad of rag that it had worn all the cold term, and he was seating a leaf of steel on its end and driving the pin home with small exact blows. The bow had a new string, and was running a lump of wax along it with his bad leg stretched out, while the boy packed, and unpacked, a load that would have done for two of him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:233:Karis waited until the bowls were empty. She had the hide on the board, spread upon Seln's oilcloth, with the brass laid along its pencil line, and a leaf beside it with eleven lines.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:279:"That is what I asked for," said Karis, and folded her leaf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:297:He read the last of it over. He did not like it, and it was true; he kept the pencil's point on the leaf beneath it for a long breath and made no mark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:23:She did not cross as Cael had known her to. In slack water she had gone over at a walking pace, set a little aslant on her two straps, so that the current itself shouldered her along the cable. This morning the current took her like a leaf. She slid down the cable's curve into its belly with the hauling rope running out behind her, faster than the gelding could back, and for a count of twenty she hung in the middle of the river with the brown water heaping against her northern side. Then she began to climb the far half of the curve, slowly, with a man leaning on a sweep; and she struck the western landing hard enough for the sound to come back across the water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:253:He turned to the other leaf, and filled a line that he had ruled twenty-eight days ago.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:273:"I cannot read them. I can tell you only that somebody could. A person sat at a desk, and gave that hole in the ground four signs, before this man ever carried a mallet down into it." She turned the leaf. There was one line of pencil on the second page, and a stroke going off toward the edge. "And here is where he stopped."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:123:She had done this once before, on the hundred and sixteenth evening, at a fire, on the day after that cord came up out of the ground. She had the figure in her trials leaf. She did not look at it until she had her new one written down, since a person who knows what she is hoping to find will find it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:125:Then she took the leaf out of her coat. *Thirty-one, and a piece under half.* The cord had not altered by the width of her pencil in fifty-four days. She did the second knot, and the third, and the fourth, and they were as the first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:153:On the trials leaf, under the figure for the cord, she entered what she had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:189:She took a clean leaf, and wrote a heading across it in her small exact hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-05.md:275:"In pieces. I had not the right to say all of it until I had looked in every other place where the answer might lie, and I have looked. It is not in fifty-two sheets. It is not in that case, or in his book." She had a leaf before her with a heading on it and eleven lines. She did not read from it. "Whoever cut those courses did not think in stone. Stone is where a thing is put when it is finished. It was written first; and what is written is kept; and the oldest keeping that I know of is on the far side of that river, inside a wall, under an office of the registry."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:21:It was the two hundred and seventy-eighth morning past the Line, and cold, with a white mist on the river that hid the cable's middle. Under the long eave, on twelve feet of tarred oak, Pike had his book open and his stone on the leaf. He was driving a tack with the butt of his penknife into the last clear space at the plank's right hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-02.md:25:The hillside rose behind the byre, steeply, for four hundred yards, to a band of grey rock that ran across it like the coping of a wall. The fence left the yard at the gate, and climbed that hill aslant, and then ran east along the foot of the rock. Teague's posts stood out of the snow the whole way up from the gate, six strides apart, as straight as a ruled line, for as far as he had got. Beyond the last of them a row of flat stones went on across the slope in the same line, each the size of a hearth-loaf, with a wisp of straw showing at its edge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-03.md:281:*Edge?* It had asked that one word of every corner and rim since a friend's sword put it in his arm, and tonight he told it yes. The lantern ran down the steel like water down a hair. He held the knife out, flat, and let a straw fall across it, and the straw lay on the floor in two pieces.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:205:It was a flat of waxed leather, as broad as a man's shoulders and no thicker than two fingers, with a strap at either end. Across the flap, in red, a stamp had been set, and three hundred miles of a wet coat had not rubbed it out. *BY SEAT COURIER. BY HAND.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:207:"It is a courier's flat," said Seln. He had leaned forward on the sill. "A seat sends its own findings in such a thing, to another seat. They are numbered. Each is signed for at both ends, and the slip comes back."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-11.md:263:"There is one sheet more in that flat," said Vastin. "It lies under the sixth row, and it is not the office's. I should prefer that you had counted the rest before you saw it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:199:Nobody started, since they were not people who started. But Karis's right hand, which had been lying on her notebook, had gone flat upon it as if the leaf might blow away; Seln's eyes had left the yard, and Lira's fingers, on the board, had drawn in. Brom's two hands had stopped on the board. And at the board's end a healer who had swallowed a sentence nine days ago, at this same table, when a station was spoken of, stood with her bowl in both hands and looked at nobody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-10.md:235:He had both palms on the board again. The fire had burned down a little, and Cael's eyes had come far enough home to show him those hands plainly at last. They were an old man's, and a clerk's, and a strap of plaited hide had been across both of them that morning. The right was raw from the heel to the fingers' roots, and the left had fared no better, and had never opened flat since he sat down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-01.md:299:On the far side of the lamp Karis had the hide flat under her four pebbles. She was not ruling anything. She had the older strip of brass laid along her pencil line, and she was sliding it up the skin a hand at a time, past the fourth ring and off the edge, onto the bare deal, as though the table might have something to say about where the line went next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:7:Vastin put his hand into the flat, beneath the sixth row, and drew out a single sheet that was tied with nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:43:Karis took up the lamp by its foot. She carried it down the length of the deal, past the tarred case and the three heaps that she had pushed aside, and the light went with her: it slid off the flat and its six rows and left them grey, and it came down the bare boards to the place where Cael's two hands lay on the table's edge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:105:There was a pocket stitched inside the flat's lid, and out of it came a sheet of thin stock, folded once. It was newer than anything else that he had brought. It had a printed head, and under the head a few lines in a quick sloping hand, which leaned a little to the right, as a man writes who is used to writing on his knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:163:She had turned to a fresh leaf. Vastin, who had begun to draw the flat's straps together from mere habit, stopped with the buckle in his fingers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:173:*Received of Vastin, at Lowmarch, day two hundred and seventy-seven past the Line: one courier's flat, holding six rows of abstracts in their tapes, one row looked through and no sheet counted; one broker's report; one page, copied by him from a register; one technician's sheet, copied by me this night and laid back in the second row. Read in part. Understood in no part. — K.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:181:He stood so for a moment, with his hand flat on his coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:183:Cael saw the thing happen from the far end of the table. The man had nothing on his back now. He had carried that flat for three hundred miles, under a pack, and a page against his chest; both were on the deal, and he had a receipt for them. He took his hand from his coat, and reached for the table's edge to steady himself, and his fingers came down an inch short of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-12.md:231:Karis had come back to her stool. She took off the gloves a finger at a time and laid them on the flat. Under the lamp lay a courier's leather with six rows in it, and a barred sheet at the foot of the second, and beside the leather a broker's paper; and at the far end, in the half dark, there lay a page on a square of oilcloth, with a title on it and a place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-07.md:211:Brom was looking at the wet letters with his feet flat on the ground; he had been Copper in a registry's book for years, and had never once cared to see his name in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:37:Brom had both feet flat on the ground. On the far end of the bench the tall man had not stirred, but his pale eyes were on the girl's left hand, which hung open at her side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:175:He walked through it. On the near shingle a flat boat lay with its bottom stove in, and two black posts leaned beside it. A board had been left face upward in the grass. The paint had gone out of its letters, but they had been cut before they were painted. *Foot. Horse. Cart, loaded.* At the head of it was the registry's seal.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:197:He was the only man within a hundred miles who had ever been such an officer. He stood at the sill in a drover's coat of brown frieze, which was too short in the sleeve and smelled of sheep; he had bought it at the last market inside the Line, and had left a grey one on its hook. He had an ash stick, cut out of a hedge. He had a pack with one strap mended in cord. Under the pack, flat against his back, lay a seat-courier's flat of waxed leather, which he had carried so for three hundred miles less a few. Inside his coat, in a fold of oilcloth, there was a single copied page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:235:A boy came along the carts a little afterward, with a flat cake in his hand, and laid it on the stump at Vastin's elbow, and went away again without a word. It was warm. Vastin had been waited upon at tables for the whole of his service, by people whose names he had taken care to learn, and he had thanked every one of them. He looked along the carts for somebody to thank, and nobody was looking back. So he ate the cake, and put a copper on the stump where it had lain. The copper was there in the morning, with the frost on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:259:It was the first valuation that anybody had put on him since he was a junior in a district house. He turned it over that night, sitting against a cart's wheel with the flat under him so that the damp should not come at it. He had been worth a seal, and a signature that a seat needed. He had been worth nine thousand findings. A man with ink on his cuffs had looked at what was left when those were taken away and had named the sum honestly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-08.md:263:There was a sheet in the flat's lid, where he had put it out of the wet, which he had taken out at a dozen halts. It had a date in it, in the office's reckoning, and he had read past that every time, as he read past the date on any return. What he looked at was its foot, where somebody had drawn a brush across a line. He knew that bar. He had not been able to leave it alone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:37:"Got it," she called. "It's slimed. Walk flat."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-06.md:45:She did not go down. She stood on that foot, with her teeth showing and the brown water piling against her thigh, until it had agreed to be a foot again; and then she set it flat, and went on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:45:The young one had stopped walking his count. On the bench Brom had both feet flat on the ground, which he did when he was pleased.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:59:He said it as he would have read a line off a slip, and he was already looking at his bow. But the tall one had not yet gone back to the eastern pegs. That long face was turned toward the bench, where a girl with her arm in a sling had her right hand flat on her knee; and it was in no hurry to turn away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-04.md:141:Brom got down on the ice like a tree being felled carefully. He had been told on a hillside what a man was worth who could not lift his right arm, and he had set forty-one posts since to find out whether it was so. He went forward now on his left elbow and his knees, with the hedging bill pushed before him, and the read told Cael what a big man lying flat did to a sheet: it spread him. The dish under him was three times as wide as it had been, and a third as deep.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-09.md:33:"No goods," said Brom. He had the purse inside his shirt, and he put his hand flat on the place. "He said so."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:23:The fist opened and lay flat, with the palm to the ground.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:31:He had gone to it without looking at his feet, at the flat of a hand thirty yards ahead of him. He stood in the wet bents at the road's edge with the stick's foot on turf, and he was not upright; he had let his shoulders come forward and his head hang a little, like a man who has stopped to get his breath on a hill. It was not well done. Anybody who knew him would have seen the effort in it. But there was nothing in that mist that knew him, and nothing in his two wrapped hands, which lay on the stick's head as quietly as they had lain on a trough.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:87:"They all know what a road is. They come up from the river quiet as a burying, two days, and never a finger lit; I've had Iron men walk through that gate who'd let a beast die in the shafts on the moor sooner than bring up the grey. They know. Then they come into my cut." A hand went out flat, and tilted. "There's a seep at the head of it. It comes down the middle of the road, a stride wide, and from the first frost till the last it's glass from top to bottom, in a place the sun never finds. A wheel goes onto that. Then the cart begins to go, backward, down a hill, with a year's goods on her and a man's own two beasts in front. What does the man do?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:131:He went over the cut itself, a stride at a time, with his eyes. Down the middle of the road ran the thing that the factor had held out a flat hand for. It began at a wet place under the rock at the head, away to his right, and it came down the whole hundred strides as straight as a rule: a stride of blue-white ice, with the dark road on either side of it. In that shade it had never softened. He knew what such ice did to a weight. He had lain on a river with his face against two hands of it, and felt a cart come up out of a hole.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:171:"They go to where it was, and it's gone, and nothing's moving. So they stand. That's her fourth line. Five hounds, or more, standing on a stride of glass at the top of a hill with their heads swinging, and us below them, as quiet as posts." He touched the pebbles. "Brom here, twenty strides down from the mark, on the south edge, where there's a flat stone to stand on that isn't ice. Lira above him on the scree. Seln over the north bank, where they'll not see him and I shan't. When they come down to try us, they try Brom first. He's wrapped."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:223:At sixty strides there was a flat stone at the road's edge, and Brom stepped onto it and set his feet, with the rock held against his thigh. Vastin stopped behind him. Cael did not see where Lira went; there was a small dry rattle on the scree above, and then nothing. Seln had left them at the foot, and was already nowhere.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-14.md:225:Brom looked at him, once. Then he put his right hand flat, palm down, at his side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:9:"I am packing," she said. "I said that I should count in daylight, on a table without elbows, and I have been awake since the middle of the night working out where such a table is to be had. It is not in this inn. So everything goes into the flat, in the order that he gave it, and the flat goes where I go."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:17:"I am not asking you to. I only enter what I see." She put the flat under her arm. "It is a hand's width that you have kept, all night. I should not have had the patience."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:39:She had said it small and clear, with the flat under her arm. Cael had known her since a library table in Ostrand, and in all that time he had never once heard her claim a job. She had put her hand up for fences and for roofs when hands were asked for, and had gone where the count sent her; she had never looked at a slip and wanted it. Pike's pen stopped over the horn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:75:It went up slowly, and a little crooked at the elbow, and it shook. But it was the first time since a white floor on the Fallow that she had voted with it, and she held it there until he had seen. Brom's left went up beside it. Karis shifted the flat, and raised her right hand with the glove still on. Seln lifted two fingers, and Cael put up his own.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:101:It took that long to leave a place where five people had lain for a hundred and forty-eight nights. Brom carried the three boxes down from the hearth's end one at a time, roped as he had roped them in the night, and slung them on the brown mule with the beckets outward, while she stood for it at the shed's door with her ears back, like a person being measured for a coat. Karis walked beside the load with the hide under one arm and the flat under the other, and would give neither of them to the mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:187:He showed them as they walked. A flat hand, with the palm to the ground, was *stand*: wherever a person was, and as he was. A shut fist was *seen*, and one looked where the fist went and did not ask. Two fingers lifted were *come on*; that was Seln's, mostly. And a hand turned over empty, as a girl had once turned hers over to him on a hillside, was *nothing*: the thing had gone, or it had never been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md:207:They drilled him for the last of the light, and it was Lira who did most of it. She had taught a boy from another crew to wait for a pole, and she went at an Archmarshal in the same fashion and with no more ceremony, walking backward before him down forty yards of rutted road with her right hand going. He stood for the flat hand and came on for the two fingers; and when the fist was thrown out to the left he looked left, and found a thorn-bush, and regarded it with great attention.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:33:He had no other words for the thing, then or later. There was no light. Nothing was thrown, and nothing went anywhere. One moment there was a road going up a hill, and eight hounds on it. The next there was a face across the road from bank to bank, as tall as the scree. He could see it only as he might have seen a pane of clean glass, by what was on it. The frost that the pack had kicked up was drifting down the cut ahead of them. It came to that place and stopped, and lay flat on nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:47:Cael had seen Paths used on floors for half his life. He had seen fire run between nine points, and a girl leave one place and arrive in another, and a Gold lay the air down in lanes; and every one of those had been a thing that went out and did something. This did nothing whatever. It was a plane, as flat as a rule and as thin as a thought. It had been drawn on the ground and stood on end. It kept the ground's own refusal to be walked through, and it would have been all one to it whether a moth struck it or a cart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:77:She was sitting on the scree, ten feet up, with her heels dug in. He had not seen her go there. She had a plate of the dark stone in her two hands, as broad as a trencher. She sent it down the slope flat, like a boy skimming a pond; and where it struck, the whole skin of the bank let go. A yard of scree went out from under two hounds together and carried them down to the road in a rattle of plates, on their sides, with their legs going.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:97:He did not lift the foot. Cael saw the boot stay where it had been set, on the line, with the whole weight of a beast as heavy as a calf dragging at the leg above it. The bandaged hands went white on the stick. From bank to bank the plane stood as flat and as still as it had stood before, with the frost lying on it. Beyond it a hound that the slide had carried down was running at the bank again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:159:The plane took it at the full stretch of its run, and this time there was no braking, since a beast cannot brake on ice with a hillside of stone coming down behind it. It struck, and dropped at the foot of that flat face, and did not stir. The others went in on top of it. They went in sideways and end over end, in a heap as high as his waist, with the plates rattling down upon them. A stride in front of his own boots, the frost on that clean face shook once and lay still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-15.md:201:It stood across an empty road, from bank to bank, with a dead hound at its foot and the frost lying flat on it. The man who held it was looking at him. He had not been able to see over the north bank, and he had not shifted a heel to try.

